  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y{[-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y[-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}\u20610]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
